.TH functrace 8  "2026-09-22" "USER COMMANDS"
.SH NAME
functrace \- Trace userspace function calls and print call trees.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B functrace \-b BINARY \-R ROOT \-f FUNC
[\-f FUNC ...] [OPTIONS]
.SH DESCRIPTION
functrace attaches uprobes and uretprobes to selected userspace functions,
records nested calls within a root-function transaction, and emits a
human-readable call tree.
.PP
The root function is included in the trace boundary. Recursive or re-entrant
root calls remain in the same trace and are represented as nested calls.
.PP
Since this uses BPF, only the root user can use this tool.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
CONFIG_BPF, BPF CO-RE BTF, and bcc. Linux 5.15 or newer is required for
uprobe attach cookies. The BPF ring-buffer support used by this tool requires
Linux 5.8 or newer.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-b BINARY, \-\-binary=BINARY
Path to the binary containing the functions to trace.
.TP
.B \-R ROOT, \-\-root-func=ROOT
Root function defining the transaction boundary. Required.
.TP
.B \-f FUNC, \-\-function=FUNC
Function to trace. May be specified more than once.
.TP
.B \-p PID, \-\-pid=PID
Trace only this process ID. A value of zero traces all processes.
.TP
.B \-o FILE, \-\-output=FILE
Write output to FILE instead of standard output.
.TP
.B \-d SECONDS, \-\-duration=SECONDS
Stop after SECONDS. Zero means run until interrupted.
.TP
.B \-D N, \-\-max-depth=N
Maximum recorded call depth. The default is 64.
.TP
.B \-B BYTES, \-\-ringbuf-size=BYTES
Set the BPF ring-buffer size. BYTES must be a power of two and at least one
page; the default is 1048576.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-verbose
Print verbose setup and libbpf diagnostics.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Trace a root function and two children as a text call tree:
.PP
.EX
functrace -b ./demo_app -R process_request -f process_request \\
    -f parse_input -f validate
.EE
.SH OUTPUT
Text mode buffers child spans until the root span returns so that each
transaction is printed as one top-down block. Incomplete buffers are flushed
when the tool exits.
.SH OVERHEAD
Tracing every selected function can produce a high event rate. Increase the
ring-buffer size for bursty workloads and check the diagnostic counters for
dropped events or depth overflows.
.SH SOURCE
This is from bcc.
.IP
https://github.com/iovisor/bcc
.SH OS
Linux
.SH STABILITY
Unstable - in development.
